  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating and comparing the skewness of the two lotteries, specifically favoring the one that is more left-skewed (i.e., assigns more probability to favorable outcomes). The relevant information includes both the size and the probability of nonzero outcomes. The individual does consider both probability and outcome size, but interprets the near-identical low probabilities as 'similarly unlikely' and then bases the decision on the higher payoff in B. This contrasts with the skewness reason, which would favor A due to its higher probability of a nonzero (favorable) outcome. The individual’s justification emphasizes the best-case outcome rather than the balance of probabilities around favorable results. There is no indication that the person assessed the shape of the distribution or considered which lottery concentrates more probability on better outcomes. Therefore, the reasoning process described does not align with the skewness-based rule, even though some relevant data (probabilities and outcomes) were considered. The justification follows a different logic.",
